<Docs
	page="recipes/rust"
	title="Rust"
	lede="sqlx works out of the box; rusqlite and postgres take a few lines of query."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Raw strings (<code>r"&hellip;"</code>, <code>r#"&hellip;"#</code>) are
			always fair game: they take newlines and quotes as written, so a query
			that outgrows its line can spread over several. A plain string joins them
			once it already spans lines, or spells a line break as <code>\n</code>.
			Its escapes come back exactly as written, and one whose SQL spans lines
			comes out as a raw string &mdash; unless an escape is part of the SQL
			itself (<code>\"Weird\"</code>, <code>'a\tb'</code>), which a raw string
			couldn&rsquo;t spell, so it stays a plain string. Any other plain string
			on one line is left exactly as written &mdash; to have squill format a
			short query, write it as a raw string.
		</p>
		<p>
			The SQL is indented the way the file is, so a crate indented with four
			spaces gets four-space SQL.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>sqlx</h2>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>rust</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query finds{" "}
			<code>query!</code>, <code>query_as!</code>, <code>query_scalar!</code>,
			and <code>query_unchecked!</code>, and the <code>query</code>,{" "}
			<code>query_as</code>, and <code>query_scalar</code> functions, however
			they&rsquo;re imported. No query file needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="rust-sqlx" lang="rust" />
		<p>
			Quoted identifiers are never touched, so sqlx&rsquo;s type overrides (
			<code>"role: Role"</code>, <code>"count!"</code>) survive as written.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				With sqlx&rsquo;s offline mode, the <code>.sqlx</code> cache is keyed by
				each query&rsquo;s exact text: run <code>cargo sqlx prepare</code> after
				the first format. And sqlx checksums every migration it has applied, so
				a reformatted old migration stops the app from starting. That&rsquo;s
				what the config&rsquo;s <code>frozen</code> line is for: squill formats
				a migration while it&rsquo;s new and{" "}
				<a href="docs/frozen/">never touches it again</a> once it has shipped.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>rusqlite</h2>
		<p>
			rusqlite&rsquo;s queries are methods on a <code>Connection</code>, which
			the default query doesn&rsquo;t look for. Point the rule at a query of
			your own, and set the dialect, since rusqlite is SQLite:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="rust-rusqlite" lang="rust" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>postgres and tokio-postgres</h2>
		<p>
			The same shape, with the <code>Client</code> method names and the default
			Postgres dialect:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="rust-postgres" lang="rust" />
		<p>
			A rule&rsquo;s <code>query</code> replaces the grammar&rsquo;s default
			rather than adding to it. A crate that also uses sqlx&rsquo;s macros needs
			their patterns in the same file; the defaults are{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/aslilac/squill/blob/trunk/crates/embed/src/lib.rs">
				in squill&rsquo;s source
			</a>
			, ready to copy.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
